    title: "How Is a Chicago Brick Two-Flat Structurally Rehabilitated?",
    description: "Chicago brick two-flat rehab starts with the structure: sagging joists, settled foundations, and deteriorated masonry are evaluated and repaired before finishes, so the building carries its loads safely for another century.",
    h1: "How Is a Chicago Brick Two-Flat Structurally Rehabilitated?",
    answer: "Direct answer: a Chicago brick two-flat is structurally rehabilitated by stabilizing the foundation, sistering or replacing sagging floor joists, repointing and repairing the masonry shell, replacing failed lintels and sills, and bringing stairs, porches, and the roof structure up to current load and safety requirements. The work is scoped from a hands-on structural assessment and permitted through the Chicago Department of Buildings, which decides approvals.\n\nChicago's two-flats were built by the thousands between about 1890 and 1930: wood-framed buildings with brick veneer walls, full basements, and the rear porches that define the type. A century of freeze-thaw cycles, deferred maintenance, and Chicago's compressible clay soils produces a familiar deficiency list. Foundations settle differentially and crack, sill plates rot where water has wicked into the framing, floor joists sag under a century of load because they were sized to older standards, and rear porches pull away from the building as their shallow footings heave. Brick lintels corrode and jack the masonry above them, and chimneys lean. The engineer reads these symptoms as a system, because a sagging floor and a cracked foundation wall are often the same problem.\n\nThe rehabilitation sequence starts with assessment, not demolition. The engineer surveys floor levels to map settlement and deflection, probes joists and sill plates for rot, inspects the masonry for bulging and failed mortar, and examines the foundation for active versus historic movement. Load calculations follow the building code's structural provisions and ASCE 7, establishing what each element must carry. Repairs are then designed to those demands: joists sistered with dimensional lumber or engineered lumber with full bearing and code-compliant fastening, deteriorated sills replaced in sections with the wall temporarily shored, masonry repointed with mortar compatible with historic brick, and corroded lintels replaced with new steel set in proper bearings. Where settlement is active, the foundation is stabilized with underpinning or helical piers taken to competent soil rather than simply patched.\n\nPorches and stairs get their own engineering because they fail differently from the main building. A rear porch is typically a wood-framed appendage on shallow footings, and the rehabilitation either rebuilds it on proper frost-depth footings with positive connections to the building or replaces it with a code-compliant stair and deck system. Throughout construction the engineer provides structural observation at key stages, since anchor installation, shoring, and concealed framing cannot be verified after finishes go on. Buildings in unincorporated Cook County follow the county's permitting path instead of Chicago's, with different submittal portals and review timelines, so the authority having jurisdiction is confirmed before design begins.",
