    description: "Cleveland roofs are designed for lake-effect snow with code-prescribed ground snow loads, drift and unbalanced load cases, continuous load paths, and drainage that handles rapid spring melt.",
    h1: "How Are Cleveland Roofs Designed for Lake-Effect Snow?",
    answer: "A Cleveland roof is designed around the fact that Lake Erie can dump feet of snow in a single event - and that the snow does not land evenly. Cold air sweeping across the relatively warm lake picks up moisture and drops it as intense, narrow bands of snow on the east side of the city, so one roof can carry far more than its neighbor a mile away. Direct answer: the engineer applies the ground snow load from ASCE 7 with Cleveland's local value, then adds drift loads at parapets and stepped roofs, sliding snow from upper roofs, and unbalanced loads on sloped and curved roofs - designing the structure, connections, and drainage for the worst of these cases, with the local building official approving the design through plan review.\n\nDrift is the load case that catches owners by surprise. Wind scours snow off an open upper roof or parking deck and piles it against any obstruction - a parapet, a rooftop unit, a step where a high roof meets a low one. The resulting drift can be several feet deep and several times the uniform snow load, concentrated on a small area of the lower roof. The engineer calculates drift height and surcharge from the code's provisions based on the upwind fetch distance and the height difference, then checks the roof framing, the deck, and the columns below for that concentrated load. Rooftop equipment gets the same treatment: a drift against a large air handler is a load the original designer may never have considered.\n\nUnbalanced and sliding loads complete the picture for sloped and multi-level roofs. On a gable roof, wind can strip one slope nearly bare while piling snow deep on the other, and the code's unbalanced load case captures that asymmetry so the ridge beam and rafters are not under-designed. Snow sliding off a steep upper roof crashes onto the lower roof below with impact the lower roof must absorb, and the engineer either designs the lower roof for the sliding surcharge or specifies snow guards that hold snow on the upper slope until it melts. Each of these cases is checked against the same structure, because the roof that is fine under uniform snow can fail under drift or unbalanced loading.\n\nDrainage and the spring melt are the final design concerns. A flat Cleveland roof that ponds water under a snowpack is carrying both loads at once, so the design provides primary drains plus overflow scuppers or drains set slightly higher - the redundancy matters when the primary drains freeze or clog with ice. Older buildings designed under earlier codes get a structural verification rather than an assumption: the engineer compares the original design loads with current code requirements and checks the actual framing, because decades of reroofing can add layers of roofing that eat into the structure's remaining capacity. The authority having jurisdiction sets the permit path - Cleveland's building department for sites in the city, Cuyahoga County for unincorporated areas - and either way the reviewer checks snow load calculations against the locally adopted values.",
    directAnswer: "Cleveland roofs are designed for lake-effect snow using ASCE 7 ground snow loads with added drift, sliding, and unbalanced load cases, continuous load paths into the structure, and drainage that handles rapid spring melt - with the building official approving the final design through the local plan review process.",
