    title: "How Do Chicago High-Rise Facade Inspections Work?",
    description: "Chicago high-rise facade inspections work through the city's facade ordinance: licensed professionals examine exterior walls on a set cycle, document unsafe conditions, and file reports the building department acts on.",
    h1: "How Do Chicago High-Rise Facade Inspections Work?",
    answer: "Chicago learned about falling facade hazards the hard way, and the city's answer is one of the strictest exterior inspection programs in the country: owners of tall buildings must have their facades professionally examined on a recurring cycle and fix what the examination finds. Direct answer: under the city's facade ordinance, a licensed architect or structural engineer performs a critical examination of the building's exterior walls and appurtenances on the required cycle, documents conditions in a report filed with the Department of Buildings, classifies any unsafe conditions, and the owner completes repairs on the schedule the ordinance and the building official require.\n\nThe inspection itself is a hands-on engineering evaluation, not a drive-by look. The examining professional gets close to the facade - by swing stage, bosun's chair, lift, or drone-assisted close observation where permitted - and systematically documents every elevation: masonry condition, mortar joints, stone and terra cotta units, window and curtain wall anchorage, sealant joints, parapets, cornices, and any ornament. Probes and sounding reveal delamination that looks sound from the ground; a hollow ring when tapped means a wythe of brick or a stone panel has separated from its backup and is being held by habit rather than anchorage. The engineer maps every deficiency, photographs it, and evaluates whether it is a maintenance item, a condition requiring repair within the cycle, or an unsafe condition demanding immediate action.\n\nUnsafe conditions trigger an accelerated legal timeline. When the examination finds a condition that could fall and injure people below - a loose stone panel, a bulging brick wythe, a cracked terra cotta cornice - the owner must protect the public immediately, typically with a sidewalk canopy or protective enclosure, and then complete the permanent repair within the ordinance's required window. The examining professional files the critical examination report with the Department of Buildings, which reviews it and enforces the repair schedule; failure to file or to repair brings violations and fines that escalate until the work is done. This is why experienced owners treat the inspection as the start of a repair program, not as a standalone event.\n\nThe engineering behind the repairs is where the long-term value lies. Repointing with the wrong mortar traps moisture and accelerates the next failure, so the engineer specifies mortar matched to the historic masonry's strength and permeability. Failed anchors get replaced with stainless or properly coated systems designed for the actual wind loads on that elevation; cracked terra cotta is evaluated unit by unit for repair versus replacement with matching reproductions; and water intrusion paths are traced to their source rather than caulked at the symptom. Buildings outside Chicago follow different rules - suburban municipalities and unincorporated Cook County have their own property maintenance and building codes without Chicago's specific facade ordinance - so the engineer confirms which authority having jurisdiction governs the site before scoping the work.",
    directAnswer: "Chicago high-rise facade inspections work through the city's facade ordinance, which requires owners of tall buildings to have a licensed professional examine exterior walls on a set cycle, document unsafe conditions, file a critical examination report with the city, and repair deficiencies on the schedule the building official requires.",
