    h1: "How Are Minneapolis Roofs Engineered Against Ice Dams?",
    answer: "An ice dam is a winter roofing failure with a simple cause: heat escaping from the house melts snow on the roof, the meltwater runs down to the cold eaves, refreezes, and builds a dam that traps the next meltwater - which then backs up under the shingles and into the house. Minneapolis, with its long deep-winter season and heavy snowpack, is prime ice-dam country. Direct answer: the engineer keeps the roof deck uniformly cold through balanced attic insulation, thorough air sealing, and continuous ventilation from soffit to ridge, so snow melts evenly or not at all - backed by self-adhering ice-shield membranes at the eaves and valleys that seal around nail penetrations if water does back up.\n\nThe physics starts in the attic, not on the roof. Warm air leaking from the living space - through recessed lights, plumbing chases, attic hatches, and top-plate gaps - heats the roof deck from below, melting the bottom of the snowpack even when the air outside is far below freezing. Insulation slows conductive heat loss, but air sealing stops the convective leaks that do most of the damage; a well-insulated attic with poor air sealing will still grow ice dams. Ventilation then carries away the residual heat and moisture: cool air enters at the soffits, washes the underside of the deck, and exits at the ridge, keeping the deck temperature close to the outdoor air so the snowpack stays frozen and stable.\n\nThe roof assembly details make or break the strategy. Insulation must run full-depth to the outside of the exterior wall - the spot where it typically thins out over the top plate is exactly where the eave stays cold and the dam forms - using baffles that hold ventilation channels open above the insulation at the eaves. Recessed lights get sealed, insulated covers; bath fans and dryers vent through the roof or gable, never into the attic, where their warm moist air would feed both ice dams and condensation. Valleys, where two roof planes concentrate meltwater, get ice-shield membrane and careful flashing, because a dam in a valley backs water into two slopes at once.\n\nThe membrane is the second line of defense, not the first. Self-adhering polymer-modified bitumen membranes applied at the eaves, in valleys, and around penetrations seal around each nail and prevent backed-up water from reaching the deck - but they manage the symptom, not the cause, and a roof that depends on membrane alone will still grow dams and icicles. Heat cables are a similar story: they can keep a channel open through an existing dam, but they do not fix the heat loss causing it, and they trade one maintenance item for another. For reroofing projects in Minneapolis, the building code's energy and roofing provisions set the requirements the design must meet, and the permit - through the city or the surrounding county or suburban jurisdiction - puts the assembly details in front of a plan reviewer who checks them.",
